T["en"] = {
    "og_locale": "en_US",
    "canonical": "https://cypherpunk.today/libretag/",
    "home": "./",
    "title": "LibreTag: an open GPS tag on the MeshCore mesh",
    "desc": "An open GPS tag on infrastructure anyone can run: Nostr bridges, and no company server in between. No SIM card, no cloud, no account. Only your keys can read the positions.",
    "og_alt": "LibreTag: a GPS tag on infrastructure anyone can run. Only you can read where it is.",
    "skip": "Skip to content",
    "nav_label": "Main",
    "nav_how": "How it works", "nav_privacy": "Privacy", "nav_features": "Features", "nav_hw": "Hardware", "nav_news": "News", "nav_get": "Get it",
    "news_h": "Latest from the project",
    "news_p": "New releases and notes from testing, posted on the project's Nostr account.",
    "lang_switch": '<span aria-current="true" lang="en">EN</span><a href="sk/" hreflang="sk" lang="sk">SK</a>',
    "other_url": "sk/", "other_lang": "sk", "other_name": "Slovenčina",

    "h1": "A GPS tag on infrastructure anyone can run. Only you can read where it is.",
    "lede": "LibreTag talks mostly to the nearest Nostr bridge, a small radio node with a program that anyone can run, anywhere. It works without a SIM card, a cloud or an account, and no company server sits in between. Only the owner's keys can read the positions. The radio link is MeshCore LoRa, and the tag goes through the wider mesh only when nothing nearby answers. When it has no GPS fix, the app shows an area, not an invented point.",
    "cta_get": "Get the app", "cta_how": "How it works",
    "status": "Status: a working prototype. The tag runs on real hardware. The app and the bridges are still early.",
    "hero_alt": "A birch trunk with a red and white trail marker and a small luggage tag hanging from a branch, over misty hills.",
    "plates_label": "Example of two tags in the app",
    "plates_cap": "Two tags as the app shows them (example)",
    "p1_name": "Car", "p1_kind": "thing", "p1_st": "Heard 12&nbsp;min ago · next ~14:31", "p1_age": "12&nbsp;min",
    "p1_ticket": "Beep · goes out ~14:31",
    "p2_name": "Backpack", "p2_kind": "thing", "p2_st": "Heard 41&nbsp;min ago · next ~14:50",
    "p2_pos": "No GPS · area ±1.2&nbsp;km (68&nbsp;%)", "p2_age": "41&nbsp;min",

    "what_h": "What it is",
    "what_p": "LibreTag is a small tag for a car, a bag or a child's backpack. It sleeps almost all the time. When it wakes up to report, it looks for a GPS fix and sends one encrypted packet, first as a quiet hello that only radios in range hear. Your app confirms it. Only when nobody confirms, or something important happens, does the report travel further over the mesh. The tag also listens for your commands at every check, then goes back to sleep.",
    "what1_h": "Radio, not a phone network",
    "what1_p": "The tag talks over LoRa with the MeshCore protocol. There is no SIM card to buy and no data plan to pay for. Most of the time it needs only a bridge or your own node within its range.",
    "what2_h": "Nobody in the middle",
    "what2_p": "Between the tag and your phone there are bridges and Nostr relays, and anyone can run any of them, anywhere. No company server holds your positions. You do not create an account. Reports are encrypted, so only the owner's keys can read them.",
    "what3_h": "Pairing by cable",
    "what3_p": "You pair the tag with a USB-C cable. That is the whole setup, and you never create an account. Later changes work over the cable or over Bluetooth.",

    "how_h": "How it works",
    "how_p": "The tag sleeps most of the time, to save battery. A wake-up that reports follows this cycle.",
    "c1_h": "Sleep", "c1_p": "Most of the time. The wake-up time shifts by about ±10&nbsp;%, so nobody can read a period from it.",
    "c2_h": "Look for GPS", "c2_p": "It waits up to 90&nbsp;seconds for a fix and notes the repeaters it can hear.",
    "c3_h": "Report", "c3_p": "One sealed packet goes out, first as a quiet hello that only radios in range hear. Nothing else is transmitted.",
    "c4_h": "Listen", "c4_p": "A short window. Your app's confirmation arrives here, and so does a command from you, over the mesh or, when your phone is near, over Bluetooth.",
    "c5_h": "Sleep again", "c5_p": "A command, if one arrived, is carried out now. If nobody confirmed the hello and something matters, the report goes out wider first.",
    "mesh_h": "Over the mesh",
    "mesh_p": "The first packet is a zero-hop hello: repeaters do not forward it, so it takes almost no shared airtime. Your own MeshCore node or a bridge in range hears it, and your app answers with a short confirmation that only the owner's keys can make. The tag floods the report through the repeaters only when nobody confirmed it and the tag moved or went quiet for too long, or when something important happens: a command answered, a low battery, a restart. The flood starts in the narrowest MeshCore region a bridge listens in, then the country's region, and unscoped only as a last step. If no repeater is heard passing it on, the tag sends one wider copy. The wider mesh is a fallback, not the normal path: a tag that stays near a bridge or your node usually does not flood at all. A phone connected to your own node hears the hello directly, and when the tag is in that node's range a command goes straight from it, to radios in range only. For now this needs our modified MeshCore Open, see <a href=\"#own-node\">Your own node</a>.",
    "nostr_h": "Through Nostr bridges",
    "nostr_p": "A bridge is a MeshCore node plus a small program that publishes what it hears to Nostr relays. It decrypts nothing and signs every report with a random key. Anyone can run one, anywhere, and the more bridges there are, the more hellos get confirmed without a flood, so the mesh stays quieter. Thanks to bridges the app finds the tag in another town or another country. When your app confirms a hello over Nostr, the bridge that heard the tag sends the confirmation. Commands travel the same way: the app leaves them in a Nostr mailbox, and a bridge that heard the tag sends them when the tag listens. The tag does that quietly at regular checks and sends nothing while it listens. Bridges announce themselves on Nostr, so the app can address a command to the ones that heard your tag.",
    "how_note": "Without your own node, the app works over the USB cable or Bluetooth (pairing and settings) and Nostr. Reports and commands then go through bridges between MeshCore and Nostr, so a bridge has to be within reach of the tag, in its country or area. With no bridge nearby, no reports arrive over Nostr.",
    "cmd_h": "Commands",
    "cmd_p": "Lost mode (the tag reports often for a day), Track for a limited time at an interval you choose, Beep, a Bluetooth scan, and Open Bluetooth remotely. Lost mode and Track can be stopped early. The tag sleeps, so a command goes out when it next listens, and the app tells you when and which way: “Beep · goes out ~14:31”, then “confirmed” once the tag answers. With your phone next to the tag the command goes over Bluetooth, otherwise from your own node if the tag is in its range, and through the bridges if neither reaches it.",

    "nogps_h": "No GPS? You get an area and how sure it is",
    "nogps_p1": "In a garage or under thick cover the tag may have no fix. Every report also carries up to five MeshCore repeaters the tag heard, and its last GPS fix with the time.",
    "nogps_p2": "The app matches those repeaters against a public repeater map (about 55&nbsp;000 repeaters, downloaded once and refreshed after 30&nbsp;days) and draws two areas, one at 68&nbsp;% and one at 95&nbsp;%. It is an area, not a point. When a repeater code is shared by several repeaters, the app says it cannot tell instead of guessing.",
    "nogps_p3": "On real data the estimate was off by about 1 to 1.4&nbsp;km. Treat it as the place to start looking. Your positions are never sent to a geocoder.",
    "map_title": "Map with an estimated area", "map_desc": "A map with four repeaters, a tag marker in the middle and two dashed circles, the inner for 68 percent and the outer for 95 percent.",
    "map_cap": "Illustration with synthetic data. Squares are repeaters the tag heard.",

    "priv_h": "Only the owner can read it",
    "priv_p": "Every feature is judged by what a thief with the device, a relay operator or someone with a radio can learn from it.",
    "priv1_h": "Sealed reports",
    "priv1_p": "Reports are encrypted in SIV mode, so a repeated counter reveals nothing.",
    "priv2_h": "A fresh tag every time",
    "priv2_p": "Each report carries a rotating 4-byte tag instead of a permanent identity. Only you can tell which tag a packet belongs to.",
    "priv3_h": "A ratchet",
    "priv3_p": "The keys advance every 64&nbsp;reports and the old ones are overwritten on the tag. Someone who steals it and reads it out cannot read the older reports.",
    "priv4_h": "A locked USB port",
    "priv4_p": "After pairing, the key leaves the tag and its USB console locks. Only your app can unlock it.",
    "honest_h": "What a radio observer can still see",
    "honest_p": "That a LibreTag report was sent, and roughly where, from the first repeater in its path. They cannot see which tag it was or what the report says. The #libretag channel itself is public. While the tag listens on Bluetooth, a phone nearby sees a device advertising from a private address, but not which tag it is, and without your key the tag answers nothing.",
    "priv5_h": "Signed commands",
    "priv5_p": "Each report points to a key that only you and the people you gave control of the tag can sign with. Bridges send only commands signed with it, so a stranger cannot get a bridge to send anything to your tag.",

    "feat_h": "What works today",
    "feat_p": "These features exist in the current app and firmware.",
    "features": [
        ("Places", "Tap the map where the tag usually rests, such as home or the garage. Inside a place the tag stays quiet: nearby radios hear a short hello, and only rarely does a heartbeat travel further. It reports when it leaves. For each place you choose whether the app tells you when the tag leaves and when it arrives."),
        ("Quiet near your Bluetooth devices", "Pick devices you carry that keep a steady Bluetooth identity, such as a ring, a watch or a hearing aid. While one of them is around, the tag stays quiet, with a rare heartbeat in case that device was stolen along with it. A phone changes its Bluetooth address and usually sends nothing at rest, so it rarely works as one of these devices."),
        ("Encrypted history", "Connect the tag to the phone by cable or over Bluetooth and the app copies the history stored on it. The transfer is encrypted."),
        ("Settings over Bluetooth", "After a restart the tag listens on Bluetooth for 3&nbsp;minutes. The app connects without a cable and can do everything the cable does, except a firmware update, a new key and a reset of the pairing. You can also skip the restart: Open Bluetooth remotely asks the tag to listen at its next check, and the app shows when that will be. It needs Android 12 or newer."),
        ("Battery estimate", "After about two weeks of data the app estimates the remaining runtime. It is an estimate. We have not measured it on a bench yet."),
        ("Low battery message", "At 25&nbsp;% the app sends you a Nostr direct message from the tag's own key. Below 10&nbsp;% the tag sends it by itself over the mesh, through any bridge. The message disappears from relays after 14&nbsp;days."),
        ("Bluetooth scan on command", "Ask the tag which Bluetooth devices are around it and it reports them. In Lost mode it includes them on its own."),
        ("Lost mode", "The tag reports often for a day. It survives a restart of the tag. If the tag stays in one place for a while, it reports less often until it moves. This is the mode that uses the mesh the most, so switch it on when you need it. Near a bridge or your own node the reports mostly stay local."),
        ("Track", "The tag reports at an interval you choose, for a limited time. Near a bridge or your own node the reports stay local. Away from one they go through the repeaters."),
        ("Fair to the mesh", "Most of the time the tag sends nothing that repeaters carry. A report is a zero-hop hello that no repeater forwards, so a tag near a bridge or your own node puts no load on the network. A flood is the rare exception: the tag moved and nobody confirmed, something important happened, or it has been out of reach for a long time. The more bridges there are, the fewer floods. The tag has a hard daily limit on what it transmits, Track and Lost mode keep a minimum gap between reports, and it still answers your commands. It does not transmit when the battery voltage is too low."),
        ("Timeline", "Browse a tag's history by day (Today, Yesterday) with a slider over its reports. The map and the status follow the slider."),
        ("Checks", "A quiet tag still wakes regularly, looks around and listens quietly for a short while, longer while the GPS searches. A command or a confirmation from your app reaches it without waiting for its next heartbeat. It transmits only if something changed."),
        ("Parked quiet", "A car that has not moved goes quiet after it is parked. Nearby radios still hear a short hello, and a rare heartbeat goes out. It reports as soon as it moves."),
        ("Your own intervals", "Choose how often the tag reports, from minutes to hours. It can also wake more often than it reports, only to listen for commands, which saves battery and mesh traffic."),
        ("Web app", "For the day you lose your phone, or when you want a bigger screen. Sign in with Nostr or restore with your recovery phrase, then see your tags and their positions, and send or cancel commands. It works only through Nostr, so a bridge has to be near the tag, and without GPS it shows no area estimate. <a href=\"app/\">Open the web app</a>."),
        ("Alerts", "Guard a spot and the app raises an alarm if the tag moves more than 300&nbsp;m. That is the only alarm. The app also tells you when a tag is not heard when expected and when its battery is low, and you choose both per tag."),
    ],

    "next_h": "In testing",
    "next_plan_label": "Ready, being tested",
    "next_plan_p": "Built, waiting for tests in the field.",
    "next_plan": [
        ("A tag without GPS that knows places by Wi-Fi", "A small ESP32-S3 board (Heltec Wireless Stick Lite V3) runs the firmware on the bench. It has no GPS. You snap a place with the tag on the cable, and from then on it recognises the place by the Wi-Fi networks it hears and stays quiet there. That works on the bench. Leaving a place has only been simulated, and nobody has run the board on a battery yet. The X1 and the Wio Tracker L1 Pro have no Wi-Fi."),
        ("Position from Wi-Fi", "A report from that board lists the Wi-Fi networks it heard. The app can turn them into a position through BeaconDB, a GrapheneOS proxy or Apple: only when you tap, in Lost mode, or for every report without GPS. Each lookup tells the service which networks are near the tag, so it is off until you choose a service."),
        ("Commands over Bluetooth", "When your phone is near the tag, it sends a waiting command over Bluetooth, and the tag looks for it at every wake-up, so the command arrives at the next check without crossing the mesh. The tag side works on the bench. Delivery from a real phone over the air has not been tested yet."),
    ],

    "uses_h": "Things and people",
    "uses_p": "The same tag, set up differently in the app.",
    "things_h": "Things: a car, a bag, keys",
    "things_ul": "<li>The tag stays quiet while a device you carry is near, because you are together. A thief does not carry your watch.</li><li>A rare heartbeat covers the case where that device was stolen with the car.</li><li>Parked quiet keeps a parked car from sending “still here” through the repeaters. Places keep it quiet at home.</li>",
    "things_limit": "It does not prevent a theft. It tells you where the thing was at its last report, and where it is now if it is still reporting. A thief who finds the tag and removes it ends the trail.",
    "people_h": "People: a child, or you",
    "people_ul": "<li>Your phone does not silence the tag, because someone could take the phone along. It is quiet only in its places.</li><li>Parked quiet is off.</li><li>In normal mode it reports at the interval you set. When someone is late or lost in the hills, switch to Track for a short interval.</li>",
    "people_limit": "LibreTag is not a certified safety device, and we make no promise that it protects anyone from harm. Tell the person who carries it. A tracker on an adult needs their agreement.",

    "cta_video": "Watch the video (8 min)",
    "video_h": "Watch the explainer (8 minutes)",
    "video_p": "A short explainer of the idea and of how a report finds its way to your phone.",
    "video_label": "LibreTag explainer video, English",
    "video_fallback": "Your browser cannot play this video.",

    "hw_h": "Hardware",
    "hw_p": "LibreTag firmware is a set of patches on top of ZephCore. Two Seeed boards matter.",
    "x1_role": "Planned, not yet run",
    "x1_p": "A card-sized tracker with dual-band GNSS, USB-C and an IP66 body. It is the board LibreTag is designed around. ZephCore supports it, but nobody has run LibreTag firmware on one yet. The Seeed listing mentions Meshtastic, because that is the firmware it ships with.",
    "x1_dl": "<dt>Chip</dt><dd>nRF52840 and a LR2021 radio</dd><dt>Battery</dt><dd>1100&nbsp;mAh (Seeed)</dd><dt>Wi-Fi</dt><dd>none</dd>",
    "l1_role": "Tested",
    "l1_p": "The board LibreTag is developed and tested on. LibreTag firmware runs on it today. It is larger than the X1 and has a display, so it suits a car or a bag better than a pocket. You flash the firmware by copying a .uf2 file onto a USB drive that appears.",
    "l1_dl": "<dt>Chip</dt><dd>nRF52840 and an SX1262 radio, L76K GPS</dd><dt>Battery</dt><dd>2000&nbsp;mAh (Seeed)</dd><dt>Wi-Fi</dt><dd>none</dd>",
    "hw_buy": "See it at Seeed",
    "hw_disclose": "Both Seeed links are affiliate links. If you buy through them, the project may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. Seeed coupon code: <span class=\"coupon\">56BZ8WER</span> (check at checkout what it covers). The L1 Pro link goes to the MeshCore version of the board.",

    "get_h": "Get it",
    "get1_h": "Android app", "get1_p": "A signed APK for now. Zapstore later. Works on phones without Google services, such as GrapheneOS. To use your own MeshCore node you need our modified MeshCore Open for now (<a href=\"#own-node\">below</a>).", "get1_btn": "Download APK",
    "get1b_h": "Web app", "get1b_p": "Runs in the browser over Nostr, for when you do not have your phone. It needs a bridge near the tag.", "get1b_btn": "Open the web app",
    "get2_h": "Source code", "get2_p": "Open source under the MIT licence. The repository is not public yet.", "get2_p_rel": "Open source under the MIT licence: firmware, app, bridges and this website.", "get2_btn": "Source",
    "get3_h": "Run a bridge", "get3_p": "A bridge is a MeshCore node on a USB cable and a small program, or the Nostr bridge app on a phone. Over Nostr the app only hears a tag where a bridge is within reach of it, so more bridges mean more places where a tag can be found.", "get3_btn": "Bridge guide",
    "get4_h": "Support the project", "get4_p": "LibreTag is free. If it is useful to you, you can support its development.", "get4_btn": "Support LibreTag",
    "soon": "soon",
    "support_url": "https://hackyourself.io/support/s/libre-tag?lang=en&amp;ref=web",
    "foot_support": "Support the project",

    "own_h": "Your own node: MeshCore Open with the Local API",
    "own_p": "LibreTag can use your own MeshCore companion radio through MeshCore Open. The app then hears the tag's reports directly and can send commands from your node. That needs the <a href=\"https://github.com/jooray/meshcore-local-api\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Local API</a>, which the official MeshCore Open does not have yet. Until it is merged upstream, use our modified build, localapi-test-10 or newer. LibreTag 0.13 does not pair with older builds.",
    "own_steps": "<li>The modified build has the same package name as the official MeshCore Open but a different signature, so Android installs it only after you uninstall the official app. Your channels and contacts are stored on the node and stay there. What goes is what the app keeps on the phone: message history and app settings.</li><li>Uninstall the official MeshCore Open, then install <span class=\"mono\">meshcore-open-local-api.apk</span> from the <a href=\"https://github.com/jooray/meshcore-open/releases/tag/localapi-test-10\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">localapi-test-10 release</a>. Its signing certificate SHA-256 is <code class=\"fp\">79750fb49349ec3729be5a3e825507a15e47c6d987e854ae91d5d50a20b3a24d</code></li><li>In MeshCore Open, connect to your companion node, turn on Local API, and in Settings turn on <i>Keep connected in the background</i>.</li><li>Open LibreTag and allow it when MeshCore Open asks.</li>",
    "own_note": "You do not have to do this. Without it LibreTag still works: reports and commands go through Nostr bridges, and settings over the cable or Bluetooth.",

    "nostr_sec_h": "Find it on Nostr",
    "nostr_sec_p": "Updates and questions go through the project's Nostr account.",
    "copy": "Copy", "copied": "Copied",

    "foot_1": "LibreTag is built by jooray. MIT licence.",
    "foot_2": "This page has no trackers, no cookies and no analytics. Fonts and images are hosted on this site.",
    "foot_license": "Open hardware, open firmware, open protocol.",
}

